    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/privacy-statement-for-job-applicants": {
      "title": "Privacy statement for job applicants",
      "description": "Privacy statement for job applicants - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Privacy statement for job applicants Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Privacy statement for job applicants This privacy statement is specifically intended for anyone applying for a job at Utrecht University (UU) or signing up for the UU job alert. UU aims to handle your personal data with care.  In this privacy statement we inform you in general terms about the personal data we process, the purposes for which we do so, your privacy rights and other matters that may be of interest to you. For what purposes does UU process my personal data? The personal data we collect from you is processed by UU for the following purposes: When you apply for a job: Assessing your suitability for the position(s) you have applied for. Carrying out the application process. Checking your references, if you have given consent to do so. After the application process: to contact you if a suitable position that matches your profile becomes available (if you have given your consent to do so). For certain positions, screening may be part of the hiring process. This screening may include verification of diplomas and requesting a Certificate of Good Conduct (VOG) or an equivalent foreign document. When you sign up for the job alert: To keep you informed of new vacancies that match the preferences you have specified. What personal data does UU collect from me? UU processes the following data about you as applicant or user of the job alert. When you apply for a job: First and last name. Address and place of residence. Email address. Telephone number. Details about your work experience and education. Other information in your CV, cover letter or other files. IP address. Where a VOG is required: whether you hold a valid Certificate of Conduct (VOG) or equivalent foreign document. When you sign up for the job alert: First and last name Email address Your preferences regarding future positions UU collects (personal) data directly from you, but in some cases, UU also receives personal data through third parties. This only occurs to the extent that it is in accordance with the law or to the extent that you have given your consent. Can UU process my personal data? UU may only process your personal data when we have a legal basis to do so. UU processes your personal data on the basis of the following principles: The processing is necessary for the execution of an agreement that we have entered into or will enter into with you. You have given consent for processing your personal data. For example, sharing your personal data with certain third parties. The processing is necessary for the representation of the legitimate interests of UU or of a third party. How long does UU keep my personal data? UU stores your personal data in accordance with the GDPR. This means that the data are not kept longer than strictly necessary to achieve the purposes for which the data were collected. After the expiration of these retention periods, UU will delete the personal data in question. The personal data obtained during the application process will be stored for up to four weeks. With your consent, we may also retain this data for up to 1 year. This may be useful if a suitable position that matches your profile becomes available at a later stage. You are completely free to choose whether you wish to give your consent for this. You also have the right to withdraw your consent at any time, without giving reasons. This has no further consequences for you or for the application process. Are you joining UU as an employee? Then we will store relevant personal data in your personnel file. Where a Certificate of Conduct (VOG) or equivalent foreign document is required for a position, you will be asked to share this document with an authorized third party acting on behalf of UU for verification purposes. This third party will destroy the copy within 90 days. Upon commencing employment, we record solely that you hold a valid document. No copy is retained by UU. The data you have provided for the job alert will be stored for a maximum of 1 year. You can unsubscribe from the job alert and/or delete your account at any time. After 1 year, you will automatically receive a message asking if you want to renew the job alert. If you do not, your data will be deleted. With whom will my personal data be shared? UU may commission other organizations to provide certain parts of our services on our behalf. If such organizations process personal data as part of that assignment, we call them Processors. UU makes arrangements with these Processors to ensure confidential and careful handling of personal data. These agreements are laid down contractually in so-called Processor Agreements. Your personal data will never be rented or sold. UU may share your (personal) data with third parties if, for example, you have given permission to do so or if this is necessary to perform an agreement between you and UU. For the application process and job alert, your data will only be shared with the processor Ubeeo, the provider of the ATS system. Your data may also be shared with subcontractors/ sub-processors who perform part of the service on behalf of Ubeeo. The privacy agreements that UU has made with Ubeeo also apply to these subcontractors. In selection procedures where a Certificate of Good Conduct (VOG) is required, your data related to the VOG application will be shared exclusively with an authorized agency, DISA, which processes the application on behalf of UU. For a VOG, this agency submits the application to Justis. For a foreign equivalent, the agency will instruct you on which document is required and how to apply for it. Your data may also be shared with any subcontractors/sub-processors carrying out part of the services on behalf of DISA. The privacy agreements that UU has made with DISA also apply to these subcontractors. Organizations involved in processing your personal data may also potentially be located outside the European Economic Area (EEA). Separate rules apply to countries located outside the EEA. Transfer may only take place to third countries with an adequate level of protection, this can be based on an adequacy decision of the European Commission, appropriate safeguards and or specific exceptions. If you would like to know more about this, please contact privacy@uu.nl . What are my rights under the GDPR? The GDPR gives you large number of rights with regard to your personal data. For example, you have the right to be informed in a timely, clear and complete manner about the processing of your data. This privacy statement is intended to do just that. In addition, you have the right to view your data and to have it corrected or deleted. In certain cases, you have the right to have the processing of your data temporarily frozen ('restricted'), the right to object to the processing and the right not to be subject to decisions resulting from fully automated processes (i.e. without human intervention) which may have serious consequences for you. And finally, in some cases you have the right to have a whole set of data that we have about you transferred to another organization. This is called the right to portability. What about giving and withdrawing consent? If we process your data on the basis of your consent, you have the right to withdraw your consent. This is always possible, even after we have already collected your data. Withdrawing your consent is as easy as granting it, and you don't have to say why you are withdrawing your consent. Please note that if you withdraw your consent, we do not have to undo what we have done with your personal data up to that point. Withdrawing your consent does not work retroactively. You can always withdraw your consent to receive the job alert via your Job application portal or by clicking “Unsubscribe” in the job alert emails and confirming this action. You can also delete your account directly in the Job application portal under “Edit status.” You can also withdraw your consent to the processing of your personal data that we retain after the application process, at any time by deleting your account in the Job application portal under “Edit status” or by using this form . Can I view, correct or delete my personal data? You have the right to know what personal data we process. At your request, we will provide you with an overview of all that data, or a specific part in which you are interested, free of charge. In doing so, we provide you with additional information, for example why we process that data, how long we keep it, and so on. We must ensure that all your personal data stored on our systems is correct. If you notice (or if you think) that certain personal data is factually incorrect, you can request that we correct that data. And because our data must not only be correct, but also complete, you may supplement data if you think the information we have about you is incomplete. In certain cases, you can do so by offering us an additional statement of fact that we will add to your file. There are situations in which you can ask us to delete certain data about you. You can do so, for example, if you feel that we no longer need this data or that we are processing it unlawfully, if you have withdrawn your consent or if you have objected to the processing. We will then check whether there are legitimate reasons to keep your personal data despite this. If there are no such reasons, we will delete your data. When can I object to the processing? In certain cases, we process your personal data because it is necessary to carry out a task carried out in the public interest or to pursue our legitimate interests (or those of another person or organisation). In such cases, we do not ask for your consent to the processing, but you can object to this based on your specific situation. If you object, we will suspend processing and balance your rights, freedoms and interests against our interests. We pay attention to your specific situation. If our interests outweigh yours, we will resume processing. If your rights, freedoms and interests outweigh ours in your specific case, we will permanently stop the processing. In either case, we'll let you know what we've decided. What does it mean that I can 'restrict' the processing? Restricting the processing is nothing more than that you can temporarily 'freeze' the processing. If you request to restrict the processing of your personal data, we can’t do anything with your information other than storing it on our systems. You have the right to restrict the processing of your personal data if one of the following situations applies: You dispute the accuracy of the data, in which case we will interrupt the processing of your data until we have verified its accuracy. The processing is unlawful or UU no longer needs your personal data for the purpose for which the data was collected and you do not want us to delete your personal data. You have, in accordance with your right to object, objected to the processing of your personal data and you are awaiting the outcome of your objection. Does UU make fully automated decisions? You don't have to accept that decisions are made about you without the involvement of a human being, if those decisions do have substantial consequences for you. UU never makes automated decisions that have substantial consequences for our employees. Can I have my personal data transferred to another organization? If we process your personal data on the basis of your consent or a contract concluded with you, you have the right to have this data returned to you in a digital common file format. You are free to pass that data on to another party. How can I exercise these rights? If you wish to exercise one or more of the above rights, you may submit a request using the privacy request form . We then have one month to respond to your request. For very complex requests (or if we receive many requests at once) we sometimes need more time (up to two additional months). We will let you know within that first month. When exercising your rights, it is necessary that we first establish your identity. We do this in a manner appropriate to the situation at hand and the right you wish to exercise. Individual assessment Please note that the rights described above are not absolute rights. We assess each request individually. Sometimes circumstances may prevent us from fulfilling a particular request. If so, we will let you know why. Questions? Complaints? Do you have any specific questions or comments about this privacy statement? Please feel free to contact us via privacy@uu.nl . UU has appointed a Data Protection Officer (DPO). This is an internal advisor and supervisor on the application of the GDPR. When you have questions about the processing of your personal data or when you want to file a complaint, you can contact our DPO via fg@uu.nl . We would like to point out that you also have the right to file a complaint with the supervisory authority, the Dutch Data Protection Authority . Contact details Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht Tel. (030) 253 35 50 Privacy statement: version and policy document This privacy statement was last amended on 2 June 2026. From time to time, changes are made to this privacy statement. Please check our website to make sure you are consulting the most recent version. Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/jobs/contact": {
      "title": "Human Resources",
      "description": "Human Resources - Organisation - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation jobs Human Resources Organisation Organisation Organisation About us Open submenu + Mission and vision Who we are Strategy Facts and figures Open submenu + Grants and distinctions Open submenu + Nobel Prize winners Open submenu + Christiaan Eijkman Gerardus 't Hooft Jacobus Henricus van 't Hoff Leopold Stephan Ruzicka Martinus J.G. Veltman Nicolaas Bloembergen Paul Josef Crutzen Peter Debye Rudolf Magnus Tjalling Charles Koopmans Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen Willem Einthoven Utrecht University in rankings Tradition and history Open submenu + Academic traditions Open submenu + Start of the Academic Year Dies Natalis Open submenu + Previous Dies celebrations Memorial day 4 May Koningsberger Lecture Lustrum University Song Awards and distinctions Open submenu + Honorary Doctorates Cultural heritage Open submenu + Utrecht Psalter University Museum History Slavery past Open submenu + Steering committee Initiatives Annual reports Sustainability Equity, Diversity & Inclusion Social safety Codes of conduct Governance and organisation Open submenu + Faculties The Executive Board Open submenu + Expenses and expense claims Supervisory Board Employee and student representation Open submenu + University Council Open submenu + Members Faculty Council Employees' consultative body Elections Open submenu + Party lists Start your own party FAQ Electoral Committee The University Labour Representation Board Degree programme advisory committee The College of Utrecht’s Assessors Network Employee (and student) representation Multilingualism in employee and student representation Professors Open submenu + Distinguished University Professors Professors by Special appointment Vice rectors Utrecht Society of Professors, Emeriti and Partners The University Service Departments Open submenu + The University Corporate Offices Open submenu + Student & Academic Affairs Office Communications Office Human Resources Finance, Control & Administration Information and Technology Services Campus & Facilities Open submenu + Sustainability Office Administrative Office Open submenu + Legal Affairs Open submenu + Administrative and management regulations of Utrecht University Office of the Board Centre for Science and Culture Utrecht University Library Talk in confidence, report or file a complaint Woo requests Collaborate Open submenu + Partners and networks Open submenu + Partners Open submenu + Erasmus+ Utrecht Science Park International networks Open submenu + LERU Collaborations Open submenu + ARC CBBC Alliance EWUU CHARM-EU Utrecht Holdings Research Support Offices Working at Open submenu + Jobs Terms of employment Professional development Collaboration Leadership International staff and guests Testimonials Practical matters Open submenu + AV & Media Open submenu + Audiovisual equipment Studios Open submenu + Greenscreen DIY-studio Podcast DIY studio Presenter DIY-studio Specials Contact Campus Open submenu + Smoke-free campus Open submenu + Smoking tolerated spots Campus development Corporate Identity Digital accessibility Facilities Open submenu + Emergencies Open submenu + Our Emergency Response Organisation Lost property Policy for demonstrations and promotional activities Room hire Open submenu + Botanic Gardens Parnassos University Museum Accessibility of buildings and grounds of Utrecht University Utrecht University Store Finance Information for suppliers Open submenu + Tender calendar Invoicing Open submenu + UU Invoice Portal (eConnect) Purchase conditions Privacy Open submenu + Data Protection Officer Privacy statements Utrecht University Open submenu + Privacy statement alumni Privacy statement campus visitors Privacy Statement Continuing Education Privacy statement employees Privacy statement participants scientific research Privacy statement students Privacy statement website visitors Cookie statement Report data breach Training courses News & Events Open submenu + In depth Dossiers Podcasts Budget Cuts Open submenu + A strong and sustainable healthy university Press and Media Contact UU home Organisation About us Governance and organisation Faculties The Executive Board Supervisory Board Employee and student representation Professors The University Service Departments The University Corporate Offices Student & Academic Affairs Office Communications Office Human Resources Finance, Control & Administration Information and Technology Services Campus & Facilities Administrative Office Centre for Science and Culture Utrecht University Library Talk in confidence, report or file a complaint Woo requests Collaborate Working at Practical matters Training courses News & Events Contact Human Resources Human Resources advises the Executive Board, as well as the faculties, on the preparation, development and implementation of corporate HR policy. Human Resources also takes care of and manages HR facilities and products which, for reasons of quality, corporate consistency, and efficiency, are organised at corporate level. Importantly, HR keeps management, staff, and HR professionals informed about these resources. In addition to this university-wide role, Human Resources provides advice and personnel management to the corporate offices. Contact information for applicants Applicants for jobs at Utrecht University can contact the faculty or service department directly for information about vacancies. Information on vacancies and applications Have you applied? In the job application portal , you can still find all the information about the vacancy, such as the vacancy text, the contact person and the status of the procedure. Is the vacancy no longer online? Then the selection procedure has started or will start soon. You will automatically be notified of the follow-up. Would you like to be kept informed of new vacancies? Then simply create a job alert. Questions about a specific vacancy? Get in touch directly with the contact person mentioned in the job description. Have you already applied? You can also find the contact details inthe job application portal after the closing date. Still not sure what to do? Then send a message to recruitment@uu.nl stating the job title. Utrecht University adheres to the national NVP Recruitment Code , developed by the Dutch Network for HR-Professionals. At Utrecht University, a (pre-)employment screening may be part of the application procedure. The type of screening depends on what the job entails and the associated duties. The screening may include a Certificate of Good Conduct (VOG), a security clearance check, and a basic verification of the information provided by the candidate. If a screening process is required for the position, this will be explained during the application process. The screening is conducted in accordance with Utrecht University’s screening policy. Human Resources (HR) departments faculties HR department Faculty of Science Hans Freudenthal Building Budapestlaan 6 3584 CD Utrecht Room 010 Telephone number: 030 253 37 95 Email: science.hr@uu.nl HR department Faculty of Veterinary Medicine Willem C. Schimmel building Yalelaan 114 3584 CM Utrecht Email: HR.DGK@uu.nl HR department Faculty of Humanities Trans 10 3512 JK Utrecht Telephone number: 030 253 62 44 Email: hr.gw@uu.nl HR department Faculty of Geosciences Vening Meineszbuilding A Princetonlaan 8A 3584 CB Utrecht Room 5.60 Telephone number: 030 253 13 66 Email: hr.geo@uu.nl HR department Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance Janskerkhof 2/3 3512 BK Utrecht Telephone number: 030 253 70 24 Email: hr.rebo@uu.nl HR department Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences Heidelberglaan 1 3584 CS Utrecht Langeveld building Room H.208 Email: hr.fsw@uu.nl Human Resources of the service departments HR Corporate Offices, University Library and University College Utrecht Heidelberglaan 8 Administration Building 3584 CS Utrecht Telephone number: 030 253 44 98 Email: ubd.hr.diensten@uu.nl Director drs. Aletta Huizenga Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/full-professor-banking-finance": {
      "title": "Full Professor Banking & Finance",
      "description": "Full Professor Banking & Finance - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Full Professor Banking & Finance Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Full Professor Banking & Finance Faculty: Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance Department: Utrecht University School of Economics Hours per week: 28 to 40 Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Apply now Utrecht University School of Economics (U.S.E.) is a young and growing Economics department. The position of the Chair in Banking and Finance contributes to the (inter)national reputation and profile of U.S.E. in line with its multidisciplinary vision on research, teaching, and impact in the area of banking and finance. The Chair plays a crucial role in strengthening the international research profile and expertise of U.S.E. and its Finance section. Your job The main goal of the Chair in Banking and Finance is to further advance research (aligned with the relevant UU strategic themes, mainly “ Institutions for Open Societies ” and/or “ Pathways to Sustainability ”), and education in the relevant fields of expertise. Apart from their own research, we expect the Chair to play a stimulating role in the USE research community; to promote a research environment in line with USE’s aims for Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion; and to attract external research funding through grants and/or consulting. Additional duties include supervision of PhD Candidates and mentoring of (Post-Doctoral) researchers resulting in state-of-the-art PhD-manuscripts and publications, and the transfer of knowledge to society by organizing events (such as conferences and workshops). Teaching of the chair will connect the field with other relevant disciplines, in line with U.S.E.’s Real World Perspective. Cooperation with other USE sections and other departments and faculties within UU in teaching and research is strongly encouraged. The tasks and responsibilities of the Chair will include: Performing and encouraging scientific research in the field of Banking and Finance; Supervision of PhD students and post-Doctoral researchers; Participating in education in the U.S.E. Bachelor and Master programs, as well as executive education; Leadership in contributing to the research and teaching climate, including stimulating and supervising multidisciplinary research and teaching; Contributing to the financial sustainability of U.S.E., including active participation in external fundraising. Your qualities The person we are looking for is a passionate and creative academic with a strong background in finance and a genuine interest in Banking and Finance. Given the current developments in this field, knowledge/research on digital/data science and AI, and the impact upon Finance – including  its role for enabling societal transitions - is highly valued. We expect the chair to have a vision on the impact of data and societal transitions on finance and the financial industry. The person should be able to integrate digital knowledge in courses in the academic Finance master programs. The ideal candidate for this position has a strong publication record and teaching experience in the field of Banking and/or Finance. The candidate is also willing and able to reach out to researchers active in other fields, such as economics and management. This requires an open and inquisitive mind, and a willingness to experiment and explore new avenues and methods in research and academic teaching. We anticipate that the candidate is willing and able to communicate complex scientific results and insights to an audience of policy makers, societal stakeholders, and the wider public; proven ability in this respect is considered a plus. The following profile will be expected of the chair holder: A doctorate in the field of finance or economics; A strong track record in research, as demonstrated among others by publications in internationally recognised journals, a high number of citations, and preferably membership of policy advisor bodies (e.g. CEPR, NBER, etc); Impact in the field, as demonstrated by e.g. editiorial roles, conference organisation, invited talks or keynote lectures, engagement with policy or professional organisations, or other forms of societal or academic impact; An interest in and commitment to multidisciplinary research and collaboration across disciplinary boundaries; Significant experience in academic leadership, demonstrated, for example, through leadership roles, the development of teams or research programmes, mentoring and supporting colleagues, or other contributions to an inclusive, socially safe and collaborative academic environment, together with a clear vision on academic leadership; Enthusiasm and commitment to the further development of research and teaching in banking and/or Finance from a multidisciplinary perspective within UU; Ability to acquire external funding; Good communication skills; Fluency in English; ability to communicate in Dutch is preferred. Our offer an employment contract for 12 months with the prospect of a permanent employment contract; a permanent employment contract; a working week of 28- 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 7.475 and €10.815 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale H2 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance is a faculty at the heart of society, with a strong focus on social issues. Our subjects of Law, Economics and Governance give us a strong mix of academic disciplines that complement and enhance each other. This is a unique combination and a mark of our faculty's strength. The Utrecht University School of Economics (U.S.E.) is part of the Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance. U.S.E. aims to contribute to an economy where people flourish by taking a broad view on welfare and its causes. We aspire to be an internationally renowned school of economics with scientifically robust and socially relevant research and education. We enrich economics with other disciplines to better solve problems and identify opportunities, from a business as well as government perspective. U.S.E.’s Bachelor’s programme Economics & Business Economics offers students a solid foundation in economic theory and methods, combined with insights from business economics, finance, entrepreneurship, innovation, ethics and sustainability. The programme has about 1,500 students from various backgrounds and nationalities and is completely taught in English. More information For more information about this position, please contact prof. dr. Niels Bosma, Head of Department Utrecht University School of Economics, at n.s.bosma@uu.nl , or prof. dr. Frank Verbeeten, Head of Section Finance Utrecht University School of Economics, at f.h.m.verbeeten@uu.nl . The structural report for this vacancy contains more information on Utrecht School of Economics and can be requested from Babette Berrocal, b.p.m.berrocalvargas-vandenbrink@uu.nl . First interviews are planned to take place in December 2026 Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion external link . If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Adam Smith Hall external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . A valid Certificate of Conduct (VOG) is required for this position. This will be requested as part of the application process. Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-position-in-generative-ai-in-health-technology-assessment-hta": {
      "title": "PhD Position in Generative AI in Health Technology Assessment (HTA)",
      "description": "PhD Position in Generative AI in Health Technology Assessment (HTA) - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD Position in Generative AI in Health Technology Assessment (HTA) Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD Position in Generative AI in Health Technology Assessment (HTA) Faculty: Faculty of Science Department: Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 30 September 2026 Apply now How can generative AI (GenAI) support Health Technology Assessment (HTA) of medicines? HTA bodies and technology developers are already experimenting with GenAI, but systematic evidence on where it adds value and where its risks lie, is lacking. In this PhD project you will map current and future GenAI use across the full HTA lifecycle, from horizon scanning to reassessment, and translate these insights into concrete, evaluated tools for HTA assessors. Your job You will be on the frontline of using and implementing GenAI in the cyclic HTA approach to medicines. Possible work topics include: Mapping current and anticipated use of GenAI across the HTA lifecycle of medicines. This may include horizon scanning and early dialogue through initial assessment, real-world evidence generation and reassessment. You will focus on how international HTA bodies anticipate it will shape their activities in the coming years. Assessing the extent to which GenAI tools can reliably extract, harmonise, and analyse information from national and EU HTA reports, and identifying the conditions required to scale this across jurisdictions. Developing how GenAI can may link the methodological needs of HTA bodies to existing and newly developed methods, in a system that is useful in practice. Examining how health technology developers are using GenAI in the preparation of national and EU HTA submissions, and what this implies for how HTA bodies assess GenAI-supported submissions. To study these questions, you will use a variety of methods, including comparative document analyses, quantitative descriptive and association studies, complex systems analyses, automated extraction data analysis and methods-needs matching Your PhD position is part of the Academic Research Network HTA, a collaboration between the National Health Care Institute (Zorginstituut Nederland (ZIN)), Erasmus University Rotterdam, and Utrecht University (UU). The Network aims to bridge HTA research and policy by supporting specific HTA research projects closely linked to policy questions. You will work closely with other PhDs in this network and interact regularly with the HTA policy officers at ZIN. Your work will also involve close collaboration with academic experts from UU and (inter)national experts in generative AI in healthcare. Finally, you will be expected to contribute to HTA teaching for bachelor and master students. Your qualities We are looking for a candidate who meets the following criteria: an MSc in pharmaceutical or (bio)medical sciences, health economics and/or HTA, epidemiology, data sciences or another relevant field; the ability and enthusiasm to learn and apply quantitative and qualitative methods within HTA; an interest in cross-disciplinary work within HTA; good oral and written communication skills in English are essential; knowledge of Dutch is considered a big advantage, particularly for communication with Dutch HTA and policy stakeholders; the ability to work independently, reflect critically on your work, and take initiative and responsibility for your projects. We consider it a plus if you have experience with: data analyses: qualitative (e.g. NVivo) and quantitative (e.g. R, SAS); using GenAI, for instance Large Language Models (LLMs) for the extraction and analysis of data as part of health research. Our offer a position for 18 months, with an extension to a total of four years upon a positive assessment in the first 18 months; a gross monthly salary ranging from €3,204 in the first year to €4,051 in the fourth year in the case of full-time employment (salary scale P under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Working at the Faculty of Science external link means bringing together inspiring people across disciplines and with a variety of perspectives and backgrounds. The Faculty has six departments: Biology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Information & Computing Sciences, Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics. Together, we work on excellent research and inspiring education. We do so, driven by curiosity and supported by outstanding infrastructure. Visit us on LinkedIn and discover how you can become part of our community. You will be employed by the Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences external link at Utrecht University, within the Utrecht Institute for Pharmaceutical Sciences (UIPS), the Department’s research institute. The position is based in the Division of Pharmacoepidemiology and Clinical Pharmacology external link and embedded in the Centre for Pharmaceutical Policy and Regulation, a multidisciplinary, internationally oriented research environment focusing on pharmaceutical policy, drug regulatory science, and health technology assessment (HTA). More information For more information, please contact: Dr. C. Leopold external link at c.leopold@uu.nl . Do you have a question about the application procedure? Please send an email to science.recruitment@uu.nl . Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace David de Wiedgebouw external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. If you are enthusiastic about this position, just apply via the \"Apply now\" button! Please enclose: your letter of motivation; your Curriculum vitae; the names, telephone numbers, and email addresses of at least two references; If this specific opportunity isn’t for you, but you know someone else who may be interested, please forward this vacancy to them. Some connections are fundamental – Be one of them #FundamentalConnection The application deadline is 30 September 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-ai-driven-urban-redesign-for-health": {
      "title": "Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health",
      "description": "Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 15 October 2026 Apply now Do you want to use artificial intelligence, geospatial data science, and urban analytics to create healthier and more equitable cities? Are you excited about developing innovative AI systems that translate scientific evidence into practical urban design solutions? Your job At Utrecht University, you will be part of the transdisciplinary ELEVATE project: Healthy living environments create healthy physical activity-related habits. As a postdoctoral researcher, you will lead the development of an innovative AI-enabled recommender system for urban (re)design. The system will combine street-view imagery (SVIs), geospatial data, mobility data, and qualitative insights to generate evidence-based recommendations for policymakers, planners, and researchers to create healthier urban environments. You will work closely with a transdisciplinary team, including social and industrial partners. Your main responsibilities include: developing an AI-powered recommender system for healthy urban (re)design; integrating multi-source datasets, including street-view imagery, GIS data, smartphone mobility data, and qualitative insights; applying and adapting state-of-the-art foundational AI and machine learning models (e.g., multimodal AI, large language/world models) with specific finetuning for ELEVATE; designing geographically context-sensitive urban design recommendations that promote active mobility (e.g., walking, cycling) related physical activity; finetuning, evaluating, and improving model performance using local data and ensuring explainability, fairness, and robustness of the model outputs; collaborating with municipalities, urban planners, designers, and residents in model co-creation and validation workshops; contributing to the development of an interactive, publicly accessible web interface (e.g., with a chat facility) for the recommender system; publishing high-quality scientific articles in international journals; presenting findings at conferences and stakeholder events; contributing to the supervision of MSc students and collaborating within the broader ELEVATE consortium. Your qualities We are looking for an enthusiastic and collaborative researcher with a strong technical background and an interest in societal impact. You have: Must have criteria: a PhD in computer science, artificial intelligence, data science, computational social science, urban analytics, geoinformatics, or a related field; experience with foundational AI model development/fine-tuning and machine learning and/or deep learning; strong programming skills (e.g., Python, JavaScript, PostgreSQL) with clear expertise in front-end and back-end development strong communication and academic writing skills; Good to have criteria: experience working with geospatial or image-based data; an interest in urban health, mobility, public space, or sustainable cities; the ability to work independently as well as collaboratively in interdisciplinary teams. Our offer We offer: a position 1.0 FTE for 12 months. After a positive evaluation, the position can be extended to 29.5 months; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 3.706 and €5.760 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information, please contact Marco Helbich external link (PI) at m.helbich@uu.nl or SM Labib at s.m.labib@uu.nl external link Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send, via the ‘apply now’ button: a motivation letter (max. 2 pages); your curriculum vitae (with links to published code and papers); contact details of two referees; one representative publication or project example. The application deadline is 15 October 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/research-technician": {
      "title": "Security verifications",
      "description": "Security verifications Additional verification required Verify you are human To use this page you need JavaScript enabled."
